  "explanation": {
    "event_selection": "The selected events are the minimal authoritative subset needed for delivery: search_object/search_zone and approach_object/approach_zone for search/approach phases, claim_<color> and received_claim_<color> for arbitration, pick_up_object and drop_object for manipulation, and the necessary visible/not_visible/reached observations for state changes. Other events are omitted either as low-level (e.g., motion primitives prohibited except in self-loops), safety/obstacle events (handled in fixed automata), or because the objective only requires single-box delivery with color-branch selection.",
    "transition_design": "The transitions enforce the protocol: search_object self-loops in the start and object_search states. When an object is visible, move to the corresponding claim state. Only in the claim state can claim_<color> be invoked, committing the robot to one color; if received_claim_<color> arrives instead, arbitration is lost and the robot resumes unclaimed search. On a successful claim, enter the object approach state where approach_object self-loops. Loss of visibility returns to search; regained visibility or reached triggers the appropriate state progress. pick_up_object only follows after an object is reached, shifting to zone search. The same logic applies for zone search/approach and drop_object ending the mission, with all observations properly sequenced and handled. Only the end state is marked, so a path exists for any eligible visible box, and exactly one delivery per robot is possible.",
